<h2>The largest market for this HS category — with an honest caveat</h2>
<p>The United States is the single largest destination for the HS 1211.90 category Indian psyllium husk ships under, at roughly USD 166 million in 2024. We're deliberately precise about what that number does and doesn't tell you: HS 1211.90 is a broad classification covering many medicinal and perfumery plant materials internationally, not a code specific to psyllium. India's dominant position in world psyllium supply is well established, and psyllium is understood to be a substantial share of this particular trade line into the US, but we use the figure directionally rather than presenting it as an isolated psyllium-only total. Full explanation on the <a href="/products/oilseeds/psyllium-husk/">psyllium husk product page</a>.</p>

<h2>What drives US demand</h2>
<p>The well-documented end use for psyllium husk entering the US is bulk dietary-fibre supplement manufacturing — psyllium is a standard, well-established ingredient in that category of product. We supply the raw graded husk or milled powder to processors and traders; we don't have a relationship with any specific consumer brand and make no claims about what any finished product contains or is certified as.</p>

<h2>FDA compliance applies here just as it does to any US food-adjacent import</h2>
<p>Psyllium husk isn't exempt from the FSMA/FSVP/prior-notice framework that governs every food-adjacent shipment into the US. FDA import alerts can place a product or a specific exporter's shipments under automatic detention pending testing — pre-shipment testing for purity and contaminants is the practical way to avoid that risk rather than discovering it at the port.</p>

<h2>Logistics favour psyllium's long shelf life</h2>
<p>At 25–35 days to East Coast ports, US transit is the longest lane we ship on at real volume — but psyllium husk's 2+ year shelf life in dry, sealed storage means that long transit is a smaller planning burden here than it would be for a perishable product. Build your reorder cycle around US transit time, not around any product-freshness concern.</p>

